🎯 What I'd actually memorize from these pages

You don't need every paragraph.

Remember these 5 things:

1. PICERL

Preparation → Identification → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned

2. Scoping is critical

Don't investigate only the system where the alert appeared. Determine the breadth of the compromise.

3. Containment ≠ Eradication

Contain limits the incident; eradicate removes the threat.

4. Preserve useful evidence

Immediately killing attacker processes can make understanding the incident harder.

5. DAIR is iterative

New information can send you back through Scope → Contain → Eradicate → Recover repeatedly.

🔥 One line to lock it into memory:

PICERL tells you the major incident-response functions; DAIR emphasizes that real investigations repeatedly revisit those functions as new evidence changes the known scope.

This is one of those SEC504 sections I'd understand deeply rather than memorize word-for-word, because scenario questions can test whether you recognize what the responder should do next.
